Only these route-local modules plus report-level claims explicitly marked canonical in `docs/state/theorem-state.yaml` are canonical Target C theorem sources at audit.59. Sections 110--121 are canonical report-level entries and are linked below. The exact train-coupled floor endpoint exclusion is also canonical; its detailed proof and independent audit are linked below. The file `95-protected-contact-creation-obstruction-audit.md` remains proof-only and is intentionally outside the canonical reading order.

## Scope rule

The §57 ZR/ZF theorem is on the nonisolated positive-contact core `V`, not the full source:

\[
\ker\Omega_V=\operatorname{span}\{\mathbf1,P_x|_V,P_y|_V\},
\qquad
\operatorname{rank}R_{C\cup H}[V]=2v-3.
\]

Contact-isolated source points remain in the global configuration. Do not extend the affine-kernel conclusion to their coordinates.

## Current route

Audit.59 retains the complete audit.52--58 stack and adds one exact source-level route barrier.

Canonical \`C-ADAPTIVE-STRICT-SAFE-SATURATION\` applies after **any** finite deletion set \(R\) has been chosen. If
\[
S\subset\operatorname{int}K
\]
is strictly farther than one from every retained source point, then with
\[
a_0=\frac{\sqrt3}{2},
\]
\[
\boxed{
E_C(R)=\varnothing
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{area}S\le a_0|R|,
}
\]
while
\[
\boxed{
E_C(R)\ne\varnothing
\Longrightarrow
\operatorname{area}S\le a_0(|R|-1).
}
\]

In particular, deleting one contact-active source vertex leaves the entire old hull interior covered by closed unit disks around the remaining source:
\[
\boxed{
\operatorname{int}K
\subseteq
\bigcup_{a\ne v}\overline B(a,1).
}
\]

Thus deleting contact-wall guards cannot create net **strict-safe area credit**. Even if the deletion set is chosen adaptively after inspecting the source, a slightly expanded triangular lattice cannot be forced by mean area to replace all deleted contact-active points, much less those points plus a diameter cover.

This does not invalidate explicit finite refill packets from \`C-EMBEDDED-WALL-REFILL\`. Discrete packings can beat mean-area guarantees through boundary effects or exact unit contacts.

The live route is therefore:

1. use audit.55 currencies and audit.58 finite motions to force a discrete blocker/wall/anchor structure;
2. repair it by an explicit finite packet, exact unit-contact exchange, or non-repacking motion;
3. do not rely on recovered strict-safe collar area after deleting wall contacts;
4. pay/delete the actual diameter cage or invoke audit.54 refill once a genuine packet is certified;
5. otherwise force an exact unit equilateral triangle.

Audit.57 remains binding for preserved short walls, and audit.54's large-wall lock regression remains binding for large fixed walls.

